1
00:00:00,391 --> 00:00:02,292
Meet Hopper, a frog.

2
00:00:02,292 --> 00:00:05,899
Hopper wants to cross a river on eight stepping stones,

3
00:00:05,899 --> 00:00:07,761
numbered zero to seven.

4
00:00:07,761 --> 00:00:10,899
Each hop goes forward one stone, or two.

5
00:00:10,899 --> 00:00:16,146
Drawn as a graph, each stone is a vertex, and each hop is an arrow,

6
00:00:16,146 --> 00:00:17,488
a directed edge.

7
00:00:17,488 --> 00:00:21,742
How many different ways can Hopper get from stone zero to stone seven?

8
00:00:28,647 --> 00:00:31,629
Let us count backward, from the far bank.

9
00:00:31,629 --> 00:00:34,363
From stone seven, Hopper is done.

10
00:00:34,363 --> 00:00:36,434
That is one way: do nothing.

11
00:00:36,434 --> 00:00:39,988
From stone six, one way: hop one.

12
00:00:39,988 --> 00:00:43,842
From stone five, two ways: hop one, then one more,

13
00:00:43,842 --> 00:00:46,612
or hop two, straight to the bank.

14
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:48,682
See the pattern?

15
00:00:48,682 --> 00:00:51,963
The ways from a stone are the ways from the stone one ahead,

16
00:00:51,963 --> 00:00:56,364
plus the ways from the stone two ahead,
because those are the only first hops.

17
00:00:56,364 --> 00:00:59,333
Stone four: two plus one is three.

18
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:02,533
Stone three: three plus two is five.

19
00:01:03,469 --> 00:01:04,602
Keep going.

20
00:01:04,602 --> 00:01:05,409
Eight.

21
00:01:05,409 --> 00:01:06,412
Thirteen.

22
00:01:06,412 --> 00:01:09,628
And from stone zero, twenty-one ways.

23
00:01:09,628 --> 00:01:13,270
Every count used only the counts of the stones it can hop to.

24
00:01:14,194 --> 00:01:17,697
In any graph, this is called counting paths.

25
00:01:17,697 --> 00:01:21,655
The graph must be a DAG: a directed acyclic graph,

26
00:01:21,655 --> 00:01:24,025
meaning arrows, and no cycles.

27
00:01:24,025 --> 00:01:29,116
To count the paths from s to t: if s is t, there is one path,

28
00:01:29,116 --> 00:01:30,301
the empty one.

29
00:01:30,301 --> 00:01:33,817
Otherwise, add up the counts of s's children.

30
00:01:33,817 --> 00:01:36,769
The children are the vertices that s points to.

31
00:01:37,752 --> 00:01:40,018
Here is the graph from your class lab.

32
00:01:40,018 --> 00:01:42,596
Vertex A points to B and C.

33
00:01:42,596 --> 00:01:45,005
B and C both point to D.

34
00:01:45,005 --> 00:01:46,307
D points to E.

35
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:48,859
How many paths from A to E?

36
00:01:48,859 --> 00:01:51,281
From B to E: one.

37
00:01:51,281 --> 00:01:53,664
From C to E: one.

38
00:01:53,664 --> 00:01:55,734
So from A: two.

39
00:01:55,734 --> 00:01:59,106
One path goes A, B, D, E.

40
00:01:59,106 --> 00:02:02,488
The other goes A, C, D, E.

41
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,383
The code asks these questions in a different order.

42
00:02:06,383 --> 00:02:08,727
To count from A, it asks B.

43
00:02:08,727 --> 00:02:10,068
B asks D.

44
00:02:10,068 --> 00:02:12,971
D asks E, and E answers one.

45
00:02:12,971 --> 00:02:14,391
D answers one.

46
00:02:14,391 --> 00:02:15,966
B answers one.

47
00:02:15,966 --> 00:02:20,094
Then A asks C, and C asks D all over again.

48
00:02:20,094 --> 00:02:22,398
D has to ask E all over again.

49
00:02:22,398 --> 00:02:24,482
The same question, twice.

50
00:02:24,482 --> 00:02:27,551
Seven calls, for five vertices.

51
00:02:28,456 --> 00:02:30,800
That repeat is harmless here.

52
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:33,144
On a bigger map, it explodes.

53
00:02:33,144 --> 00:02:37,987
This is a hiking map: seven junctions, twelve one-way trails,

54
00:02:37,987 --> 00:02:39,172
all uphill.

55
00:02:39,172 --> 00:02:42,476
How many different routes lead from Start to Summit?

56
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:53,329
Plain recursion asks each child, and each child asks its own children,

57
00:02:53,329 --> 00:02:54,657
with no memory.

58
00:02:54,657 --> 00:02:56,558
Watch the calls pile up.

59
00:02:56,558 --> 00:03:00,370
Twenty-four calls, for a map with seven junctions.

60
00:03:02,394 --> 00:03:03,839
Look at the repeats.

61
00:03:03,839 --> 00:03:06,352
Meadow is asked five times.

62
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:08,813
Falls is asked four times.

63
00:03:08,813 --> 00:03:13,510
Every time, the answer is one, recomputed from scratch.

64
00:03:14,454 --> 00:03:18,777
The fix: write each answer down the first time, and look it up after.

65
00:03:18,777 --> 00:03:22,670
That is called memoization, and the table is a cache.

66
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:25,587
Here is the same search with a cache.

67
00:03:25,587 --> 00:03:29,636
Whenever a junction was already answered, it is a cache hit:

68
00:03:29,636 --> 00:03:32,055
read the table, and stop there.

69
00:03:33,018 --> 00:03:35,453
Here is the table filling in.

70
00:03:35,453 --> 00:03:37,197
Summit: one.

71
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:38,656
Meadow: one.

72
00:03:38,656 --> 00:03:40,336
Falls: one.

73
00:03:40,336 --> 00:03:42,081
Creek: two.

74
00:03:42,081 --> 00:03:43,773
Ridge: two.

75
00:03:43,773 --> 00:03:45,362
Pine: five.

76
00:03:45,362 --> 00:03:47,042
Start: nine.

77
00:03:47,042 --> 00:03:48,305
Nine routes.

78
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:52,272
Each junction was finished only after all of its children.

79
00:03:53,208 --> 00:03:54,367
Count the work.

80
00:03:54,367 --> 00:03:57,857
Each junction is computed once: seven.

81
00:03:57,857 --> 00:04:02,479
Each trail is looked at once, when its starting junction is computed:

82
00:04:02,479 --> 00:04:03,442
twelve.

83
00:04:03,442 --> 00:04:08,364
So there are thirteen calls: one for the start, plus one per trail.

84
00:04:08,364 --> 00:04:11,147
That is V plus E: linear time.

85
00:04:12,071 --> 00:04:13,321
Now your code.

86
00:04:13,321 --> 00:04:16,967
The first line: from functools import cache.

87
00:04:16,967 --> 00:04:21,612
Functools is a toolbox that comes with Python, and cache is one tool in it.

88
00:04:22,545 --> 00:04:27,207
Def count_paths takes the graph, a start s, and a target t.

89
00:04:27,207 --> 00:04:32,411
The last line, return nested of s, starts the whole search from the start.

90
00:04:33,331 --> 00:04:36,209
The line above the inner def is at cache.

91
00:04:36,209 --> 00:04:39,907
The at sign means: wrap the function underneath.

92
00:04:39,907 --> 00:04:42,901
The wrapped function keeps a table of answers.

93
00:04:42,901 --> 00:04:47,498
The first time it sees an input, it runs, and writes the answer down.

94
00:04:47,498 --> 00:04:50,792
The next time it sees the same input, it skips the function,

95
00:04:50,792 --> 00:04:52,533
and returns what is written.

96
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:59,117
Nested is a function inside a function,
so it can read graph and t from outside.

97
00:04:59,117 --> 00:05:02,867
A new nested is built every time count_paths is called,

98
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:05,602
so its table starts empty each time.

99
00:05:05,602 --> 00:05:11,162
That is why the table can be keyed by s
alone, even though the answer depends on t:

100
00:05:11,162 --> 00:05:14,039
inside one call, t never changes.

101
00:05:14,039 --> 00:05:18,827
The s in the parentheses is a fresh variable: this call's vertex.

102
00:05:19,767 --> 00:05:22,970
If s equals t, return one.

103
00:05:22,970 --> 00:05:27,502
Arriving at the target is exactly one path, the empty path.

104
00:05:27,502 --> 00:05:30,792
The parentheses around the comparison are optional.

105
00:05:31,692 --> 00:05:33,528
Now the densest line.

106
00:05:33,528 --> 00:05:37,838
Return sum of nested of child, for child in graph of s.

107
00:05:37,838 --> 00:05:39,088
Take it apart.

108
00:05:39,088 --> 00:05:41,927
Graph of s is the list of children of s.

109
00:05:41,927 --> 00:05:45,660
At vertex A in the diamond, that is B and C.

110
00:05:46,595 --> 00:05:50,111
For child in graph of s means: go through that list,

111
00:05:50,111 --> 00:05:51,830
one child at a time.

112
00:05:51,830 --> 00:05:54,720
Nested of child is the count for that child.

113
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:57,962
Together they produce one number for each child.

114
00:05:57,962 --> 00:06:02,620
Wrapped in parentheses like this, it is called a generator expression.

115
00:06:03,562 --> 00:06:05,619
Sum adds those numbers up.

116
00:06:05,619 --> 00:06:10,893
If s has no children, there is nothing to add, and the sum of nothing is zero.

117
00:06:10,893 --> 00:06:15,450
Here is the same thing written longhand: a total that starts at zero,

118
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:17,530
a loop, and a return.

119
00:06:18,398 --> 00:06:19,963
Run it on the diamond.

120
00:06:19,963 --> 00:06:25,431
A asks B, B asks D, D asks E, and E returns one.

121
00:06:25,431 --> 00:06:26,603
D is stored.

122
00:06:26,603 --> 00:06:27,801
B is stored.

123
00:06:27,801 --> 00:06:31,043
Then A asks C, and C asks D.

124
00:06:31,043 --> 00:06:33,791
A cache hit: one, instantly.

125
00:06:33,791 --> 00:06:35,080
C is stored.

126
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:38,566
And A adds one plus one: two.

127
00:06:39,484 --> 00:06:42,245
Your third test asks for E to A.

128
00:06:42,245 --> 00:06:47,594
Nested of E: E is not A, and E has no children, so the sum of nothing is zero.

129
00:06:47,594 --> 00:06:48,945
Zero paths.

130
00:06:49,862 --> 00:06:52,063
One rule: no cycles.

131
00:06:52,063 --> 00:06:57,623
Suppose vertex A pointed to vertex B, B to C, and C back to A.

132
00:06:57,623 --> 00:07:02,792
Then nested of A would call B, which calls C, which calls A again,

133
00:07:02,792 --> 00:07:03,781
forever.

134
00:07:03,781 --> 00:07:06,255
Python gives up with a recursion error.

135
00:07:06,255 --> 00:07:08,964
That is why the graph must be acyclic.

136
00:07:08,964 --> 00:07:14,416
In the book's words, the dependency
graph of a recurrence must have no cycles.

137
00:07:15,336 --> 00:07:20,024
Now your last test: sixty stepping stones, hop one or two.

138
00:07:20,024 --> 00:07:22,771
The test builds the graph in one line.

139
00:07:22,771 --> 00:07:27,211
For i in range sixty, make a key i, with the list i plus one,

140
00:07:27,211 --> 00:07:28,500
i plus two.

141
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:33,982
The vertical bar merges two dictionaries,
here adding stones sixty and sixty-one,

142
00:07:33,982 --> 00:07:35,684
which have no hops.

143
00:07:36,612 --> 00:07:38,097
How many ways?

144
00:07:38,097 --> 00:07:42,771
Two trillion, five hundred four billion, seven hundred thirty million,

145
00:07:42,771 --> 00:07:46,951
seven hundred eighty-one thousand, nine hundred sixty-one.

146
00:07:46,951 --> 00:07:51,951
Those are Fibonacci numbers, like the ones
from the dynamic programming reading:

147
00:07:51,951 --> 00:07:54,447
each count is the sum of the two ahead.

148
00:07:55,377 --> 00:07:58,671
Plain recursion would make over eight trillion calls.

149
00:07:58,671 --> 00:08:02,760
At ten million calls a second, that is more than nine days.

150
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:06,015
With the cache: one hundred twenty-one calls.

151
00:08:06,015 --> 00:08:08,160
A fraction of a millisecond.

152
00:08:09,052 --> 00:08:13,531
This is memoization, and it is depth-first search in disguise.

153
00:08:13,531 --> 00:08:15,784
Each junction is a vertex.

154
00:08:15,784 --> 00:08:18,648
Each call that needs another is an edge.

155
00:08:18,648 --> 00:08:20,979
Storing an answer is marking.

156
00:08:20,979 --> 00:08:24,846
Computing the value happens after the children, at the post-visit.

157
00:08:24,846 --> 00:08:30,133
The book puts it in one line: memoized recursion
is depth-first search of the dependency

158
00:08:30,133 --> 00:08:31,028
graph.

159
00:08:31,973 --> 00:08:33,184
To recap.

160
00:08:33,184 --> 00:08:39,538
Paths from s to t: one if s is t, otherwise the sum over the children.

161
00:08:39,538 --> 00:08:41,648
Plain recursion repeats work.

162
00:08:41,648 --> 00:08:46,635
A cache answers each vertex once, so the time is V plus E.

163
00:08:46,635 --> 00:08:49,239
And the answers come out children first.

164
00:08:49,239 --> 00:08:53,649
Next video: the other half of your lab, topological order.
